KickOS: newlib's locks as KickOS locks, each FILE with its own, and the orders a split takes.

Against newlib 4.5.0.20241231, applied with -p1 in the newlib tree after any family patch, for
every family. With retargetable locking, struct __lock is complete: the KickOS mutex word, then
the depth its holder has taken it to. A static lock stays a named object of that type and a
FILE's lock is one held in the FILE, which _flockfile and _funlockfile pass by address. A FILE's
lock is unheld from its storage's making and never set again, so neither opening nor closing a
FILE touches it; only a FILE on the stack sets its own. __fp_lock_all takes sfp, then tries the
lock of every FILE slot, and on one held elsewhere lets all go, waits for that one and starts
again, so no FILE opens, closes or reopens until __fp_unlock_all. __call_exitprocs runs each
function without the atexit lock and reads the table again after it, and clears a removed slot's
kind. The headers state the monotonic
clock and declare clock_gettime and nanosleep, which the KickOS user runtime defines, without the
POSIX timers, and _reclaim_reent names the global reent rather than _impure_ptr. This modifies the
vendored C library, so it carries newlib's licence, not KickOS's.

